import CombinatorialGroupTheory_Magnus_2004.Chap02.Proposition_2_5_31

-- Declarations for this item will be appended below by the statement pipeline.

universe u v

open scoped Monoid.Coprod

set_option autoImplicit false

section

variable {X : Type u} [Group X] [Nontrivial X]
variable {Y : Type v} [Group Y] [Nontrivial Y]

-- Layer triage:
-- `source-facing`: a nontrivial free product `X ∗ Y` together with the exclusion of the unique
-- exceptional case where both factors have order `2`.
-- `core/canonical`: `Monoid.Coprod` with notation `X ∗ Y` is mathlib's owner abstraction for the
-- free product of two groups, `Nat.card` is the project's standard universe-stable notion of group
-- order, and `IsSQUniversal` from Proposition `2-5-31` is the owner predicate for
-- `SQ`-universality.
-- `bridge/view`: no auxiliary bridge is needed here, since the textbook statement is already
-- about the canonical free-product owner object itself.
-- Domain sampling:
-- 1. `Monoid.Coprod` with notation `X ∗ Y` is the existing project-wide owner for free products
--    of two groups.
-- 2. Proposition `2-5-31` introduces `IsSQUniversal` as the canonical subquotient-universality
--    predicate on a group.
-- 3. Proposition `3-12-3` records the same exceptional branch for free products by the condition
--    `¬ (Nat.card G₁ = 2 ∧ Nat.card G₂ = 2)`, so that is the established project encoding of
--    “not the free product of two copies of `C₂`”.
-- Primitive vs. derived:
-- the primitive public data are only the two nontrivial factor groups and the exclusion of the
-- order-`2`/order-`2` case; `SQ`-universality is the resulting owner-level property of the free
-- product.

-- Proof sketch: use the classical small-cancellation construction from the textbook. For any
-- countable group `H`, adjoin `H` as a free factor to `X ∗ Y`, impose the symmetrized relators
-- from the theorem text to obtain a `C'(1/10)` small-cancellation quotient, and apply the
-- embedding theorem for factors in a small-cancellation product. The quotient is generated by the
-- images of `X` and `Y`, hence is a quotient of `X ∗ Y`, so `H` embeds in a quotient of a
-- subgroup of `X ∗ Y`.
/-- Theorem 5-10-3: if `X` and `Y` are nontrivial and are not both of order `2`, then the free
product `X ∗ Y` is `SQ`-universal. -/
theorem isSQUniversal_freeProduct_of_nontrivial_of_not_both_order_two
    (hnot_order_two : ¬ (Nat.card X = 2 ∧ Nat.card Y = 2)) :
    IsSQUniversal (X ∗ Y) := sorry

end
